    <body>

        <div xml:id="AN208.1-kilavi">
          <p type="edition">puṇarntu nīṅkun talaimakaṉ ṟaṉṉeñciṟkuc colliyatu.</p>
          <p type="translation" xml:lang="en">What HE said to his heart when leaving after the union.</p>
        </div>
        <div rend="parallel">
      <lg xml:id="AN208.1">
        <l>yāma viravi ṉeṭuṅkaṭai niṉṟu</l>
        <l>tēmutir cimaiya kuṉṟam pāṭu</l>
        <l>nuṇkō lakavu<supplied>na</supplied>r vēṇṭiṉ veṇkōṭ</l>
        <l>ṭaṇṇal yāṉai yīyum vaṇmakiḻ</l>
        <l>veḷiyaṉ veṇmā ṉāa yeyiṉa</l>
        <l>ṉaḷiyiyal vāḻkkaip pāḻip paṟantalai</l>
        <l>yiḻaiyaṇi yāṉai yiyaṟēr miñiliyoṭu</l>
        <l>naṇpaka luṟṟa ceruviṟ puṇkūrn</l>
        <l>toḷvāṇ mayaṅkamar <supplied>vī</supplied>ḻnteṉap puḷḷoruṅ</l>
        <l>kaṅkaṇ vicumpiṉ viḷaṅku ñāyiṟ</l>
        <l>ṟoṇkatir te<supplied>ṟā</supplied>maṟ ciṟakariṟ kōli</l>
        <l>niḻalcey tuḻaṟal kāṇeṉac ciṉai<supplied>i</supplied>p</l>
        <l>paṭukaḷaṅ kāṇṭal cellāṉ ciṉañciṟan</l>
        <l>turuviṉai na<supplied>ṉṉa</supplied> ṉaruḷaṉ cāyap</l>
        <l>peruvitup puṟṟa palvēṇ makaḷir</l>
        <l>kurū<supplied>u</supplied>ppūm paintā rarukkiya pūcal</l>
        <l>varaiviṭak kaṭakkum vayaṅkuperun tāṉai</l>
        <l>yakutai taḷa<supplied>rvu</supplied>tī<supplied>r</supplied>n tāṅku mikupeya</l>
        <l>luppucciṟai nillā veḷḷam pōla</l>
        <l>nāṇuvarai nillāk kāma naṇṇi</l>
        <l>nalkiṉaḷ vāḻiyar vantē yōri</l>
        <l>palpaḻap palaviṉ payaṅkeḻu kollik</l>
        <l>kārmalar kaṭuppa nāṟu</l>
        <l>mērnuṇ ṇōti māa yōḷē.</l>
      </lg>
      <lg type="translation" xml:lang="en">
        <l corresp="19">Like a flood that does not stop before a salt ridge</l>
        <l corresp="18">in the ample rain, as if the slackening of Akutai had ended,</l>
        <l corresp="17">with a shining big army that excels, beyond the limit,</l>
        <l corresp="16">in a clamour, which makes brilliant flowers [meant] for fresh garlands scarce,</l>
        <l corresp="15">of many ladies of the Vēḷ [clan] who felt great haste,</l>
        <l corresp="14">when merciless Naṉṉaṉ of fearful work died,</l>
        <l corresp="13">exceeding in [his] anger, he who could  not see the battle field,</l>
        <l corresp="12">as a disturbance arose – look – throwing a shadow,</l>
        <l>as birds’ wings together interlaced without being scorched</l>
        <l corresp="9d-11">by the bright beams of the sun shining in the fine-spaced(?) sky,</l>
        <l corresp="9a-c">as they descended on the confuse battle of bright swords,</l>
        <l corresp="8">wounds abounding in the fight that took place at high noon,</l>
        <l corresp="7">with Miñili on a chariot moving forward with a jewel-adorned elephant,</l>
        <l corresp="6">by the desert village of Pāḻi, with a living of caring nature,</l>
        <l corresp="5">Āay, the hunter, with white horses, Veḷiyaṉ,</l>
        <l corresp="4">with liberal toddy, who gives superior elephants</l>
        <l corresp="3">with white tusks, when singers with fine staffs want [them],</l>
        <l corresp="2">who sing of the hill with honey-ripening peaks,</l>
        <l corresp="1">standing [there] at the long edge of midnight</l>
        <l>coming, she drew near [and] granted – may she live –</l>
        <l corresp="20-21c">passion that does not stop before the boundary of shame,</l>
        <l corresp="24">the dark one with pretty fine hair,</l>
        <l corresp="23">fragrant so as to resemble blossoms in monsoon time</l>
        <l corresp="22">from Kolli [hill] rich in the yield of jackfruit trees with many fruits.</l>
      </lg>
        </div>
